\begin{definition} \label{deffgp}
  The full Galvin number. \newline
  Let \mathfrak{fgp} be the first \lambda for which every family \mathcal{C}=\{C_\alpha:\alpha\in\lambda^+\}\subseteq\mathscr{D}_{\aleph_1} contains a subfamily \mathcal{D} of size \lambda^+ such that \bigcap\mathcal{D}\in\mathscr{D}_{\aleph_1}.
\end{definition}

nodes.raw_latex:
\begin{definition} \label{deffgp}
  The full Galvin number. \newline
  Let \mathfrak{fgp} be the first \lambda for which every family \mathcal{C}=\{C_\alpha:\alpha\in\lambda^+\}\subseteq\mathscr{D}_{\aleph_1} contains a subfamily \mathcal{D} of size \lambda^+ such that \bigcap\mathcal{D}\in\mathscr{D}_{\aleph_1}.
\end{definition}

By a result of Shelah, if $2^\mu>\mu^+$ then there is a good scale at $\mu^+$, see \cite{MR1318912} or \cite[Section 19]{MR2160657}.
Thus, if $\mathfrak{gp}=\mu$ then necessarily $2^\mu=\mu^+$.
Likewise, if $2^{\aleph_0}<\mu$ then $\mathfrak{gp}\leq 2^{\aleph_0}<\mu$.
Hence, if $\mathfrak{gp}=\mu$ then $2^{\aleph_0}\geq\mu$ and then necessarily $2^{\aleph_0}\geq\mu^+$.
Since $2^{\aleph_0}\leq 2^\mu=\mu^+$, we are done.

\hfill \qedref{rcountablecof}

